# Type Embedding and Ambiguity Resolution in Go 

         Type embedding is a fundamental feature of the Go programming language. This feature allows one type to be embedded within another, effectively promoting the embedded type's fields and methods to the outer type. 

         ```go 

         type employee struct { 

            name string 

            surname string 

         } 

         type manager struct { 

            employee 

            department string 

         } 

         ``` 

         However, this feature can sometimes lead to ambiguities. For instance, if both the `employee` and `manager` types have a method with the same name, it can be difficult to determine which method is being called. 

         ```go 

         func (e employee) greet() { 

            fmt.Println("Hello", e.name) 

         } 

         func (m manager) greet() { 

            fmt.Println("Hello", m.name, "from", m.department) 

         } 

         ``` 

         In such cases, the Go language's ambiguity resolution rules come into play. These rules help determine which method is being called, resolving the ambiguity by promoting the method from the innermost type if there's a conflict.

