# Mastering Heap Allocations in Golang
Heap allocations are a critical concept in Golang that helps you write high-performance code. This allocation tracks the lifetime of variables and determines which ones can be stored on the stack and which ones are allocated on the heap.
## How Heap Allocations Work
Heap allocations occur at compile time. The compiler analyzes your code and determines the lifetime of variables. If a variable is referenced outside the function, it is allocated on the heap.
### Example Code
```go
package main

import "fmt"

type Data struct {
   Name string
   Surname string
}

func NewData() *Data {
   return &Data{"Example", "Name"}
}

func main() {
   data := NewData()
   fmt.Println(data)
}
```
In this example, the `NewData` function returns a `Data`-type variable. This variable is referenced in the `main` function. Therefore, this variable is allocated on the heap.
## Why Heap Allocations Matter
Heap allocations are a critical tool for optimizing your code. Variables allocated on the heap can cause memory leaks. Heap allocations help prevent these issues.
### Example Application
Heap allocations are used to write high-performance code. For example, when developing a web server, you can optimize your code using heap allocations.
```go
package main

import (
   "net/http"
   "sync"
)

type Data struct {
   Name string
   Surname string
}

var dataMu *sync.Mutex = &sync.Mutex{}

func NewData() *Data {
   dataMu.Lock()
   defer dataMu.Unlock()
   return &Data{"Example", "Name"}
}

func main() {
   http.HandleFunc("/", func(w http.ResponseWriter, r *http.Request) {
      data := NewData()
      fmt.Fprint(w, data)
   })
   http.ListenAndServe(":8080", nil)
}
```

